{"questions":[{"question_id":"5urq95W4yMPfUd4T","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2004","year":2004,"examDate":2004,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"hybridization-and-vsepr-theory","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"which-one-of-the-following-has-the-regular-tetrahedral-struc-2004-marks-4-5urq95w4ympfud4t.htm","content":"Which one of the following has the regular tetrahedral structure? <br>\n\n(Atomic nos : B = 5, S = 16, Ni = 28, Xe = 54)","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"XeF<sub>4</sub>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"[Ni(CN)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2- </sup>"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.69ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.969ex\" height=\"2.554ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -823.8 2196.4 1128.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\" transform=\"translate(759,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D439\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D439\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(837.3,411.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(676,-305.1) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"SF<sub>4</sub>"}],"correct_options":["C"],"explanation":"Regular Tetrahedral structure is possible in sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization where central atom has 4 bond pair and no lone pair.\n<br><br>(a)   XeF<sub>4</sub>  is sp<sup>3</sup>d<sup>2</sup> hybridised and structure is square planar.\n<br><br><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/yCYzjWm1BbDJweRNh/nLQlDvxoOOeu04IjbnCTHFHShaMBc/BLQODeVT8TdK7EuTiJa5zU/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 1\">\n<br><br>(b)   [Ni(CN)<sub>4</sub><sub></sub>]<sup><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>2</sup> is coordinate compound and oxidation number of Ni is +2.\n<br><br>Electronic configuration of Ni<sup>+2</sup> is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0.301ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"0.529ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -367 778 234\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> [Ar]3d<sup>8</sup>\n<br><br><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/KIpkwniZxdg216fv4/E8CRilJKk3ZTsRFwav3CskKlQ26CT/KN0hCT16TVTHJEyIi6MEx6/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 2\">\n<br><br>But  because of CN<sup><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></sup> ion which  is a strong  field ligand , it can perform pairing of electron.\n<br><br><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/hvhkJdHtMQ2yT5uJU/Td6zlq3BkFPH15WPxRgT6IsKFwUQY/9wLsh4Q5zFzlXZw2rP2V0p/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 3\">\n<br><br>And the structure of dsp<sup>2</sup> hybridization is square planar.\n<br><br><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/x9a7YjYDD0tFEpHyV/mYJu36FbvU0v3FdT8eQkgk3NexYeY/MsoK0qJfSZTEH8shYl5ohd/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 4\">\n<br><br>(C) <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.511ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 668 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(501,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> BF<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.69ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.432ex\" height=\"2.554ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -823.8 633.1 1128.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(33,411.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(33,-305.1) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, 4 bond pair present so angle is 109<sup>o</sup>  28<sup>'</sup> and sp<sup>3</sup> hybridised. So structure is regular tetrahedral.\n<br><br><img class=\"question-image\" src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/cSH8ohR2lpBHQKDAE/i0QcsYhcoqZkW3Gy6Pwqtyt4n6FLb/gHbLsODqAP31gaL9WAura2/image.png\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 5\">\n<br><br>(d)   <img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/a5XQH5opv8YSzognp/YvymIZGBuBz4gsj9qPyRlAdwh4teU/UsvHEhTmbRnd0ERrbYqXOu/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2004 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 257 English Explanation 6\">\n<br><br>SF<sub>4</sub> is sp<sup>3</sup>d hybridised and structure is see-saw."}]}